Footnote: ⁎ [Here, again, as on pp. 23, 24, I have, in the translation, been necessarily obliged to deviate slightly from the original. *Klang*, as here defined, embraces *Ton* as a particular case. @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
I use *tone* for the general term, and *compound tone* and *simple tone* for the two particular cases. Thus, as presently mentioned in the text, the *tone* produced by a tuning-fork held over a proper resonance chamber we know, @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
on analysis, to be *simple*, but before analysis it is to us only a (musical) *tone* like any other, and hence in this case the Author's *Klang* becomes the Author's *Ton*. @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
I believe that the language used in my translation is best adapted for the constant accurate distinction between compound and simple tones by English readers, @@FN@@
>>>
<<<
@@FN@@
===
as I leave nothing which runs counter to old habits, and by the use of the words simple and compound, constantly recall attention to this newly discovered and extremely important relation.—*Translator*.]

@@FN@@
